---
/**
 * The CLI (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §15: "Document supported setups and errors").
 *
 * Every claim here is read from `packages/cli/src` — the exit codes from
 * `commands.ts` (`EXIT`), the messages from where they are emitted, and the
 * guarantees from the headers of `fs-safe.ts` and `manifest.ts`. The refusals
 * are quoted rather than paraphrased, because a reader searching an error
 * message has to find the same string here.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout
  title="The CLI"
  description="Install, update, and troubleshoot Santaka UI components with the santaka CLI."
>
  <h1>The CLI</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    <code>santaka</code> copies component source into your project. It reads JSON, writes files, and
    runs nothing: there is no field in the registry schema that names a script, and the CLI never
    invokes your package manager.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Install modes</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Source mode</strong> is the default and the mode this guide is about: the files the
      registry entry describes are copied into your repository, so you own them from that moment on.
      Every file is checked against the checksum the entry declares before it is written.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Package mode</strong> prints the install commands for the published packages instead
      of running them. A scaffolder that shells out to a package manager cannot tell a network
      failure from a permissions failure from a version conflict, so it does not try.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Supported setups</h2>
    <p>
      <code>init</code> and <code>add</code> detect the project from the files in the directory they
      run against, and report the evidence for each conclusion rather than asking you to trust it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>What</th><th>Detected from</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Framework</td>
          <td>
            <code>react-vite</code>, <code>nextjs</code>, <code>vue</code>, <code>nuxt</code>,
            <code>svelte</code>, <code>astro</code>, or <code>vanilla</code>, from dependencies and
            config files.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Package manager</td>
          <td><code>pnpm</code>, <code>npm</code>, <code>yarn</code>, or <code>bun</code>, from the lockfile. With no lockfile in sight it says so and assumes <code>npm</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>TypeScript</td>
          <td>A <code>tsconfig.json</code>, or a <code>typescript</code> dependency.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Styling</td>
          <td>Whatever styling setup it finds, reported as evidence.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>--framework &lt;name&gt;</code> overrides detection without silencing it — the report
      still shows what was found and what was overridden. An unknown value fails with the supported
      list rather than guessing a near match.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Commands</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>init</code></td>
          <td>Detects the framework, reports the evidence, and prints the install commands for the chosen mode.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>add &lt;name…&gt;</code></td>
          <td>Copies source for a component or a block. Resolves <code>registryDependencies</code> first — adding a block installs every component it composes — refuses paths that escape the project, verifies checksums, and stops on a conflict.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>list</code></td>
          <td>Lists the available components and blocks, with counts by type, and what is already installed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>doctor</code></td>
          <td>Reports what the CLI can see in the project and what looks wrong.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>diff [component]</code></td>
          <td>Compares installed files against the manifest and prints a unified diff.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>update</code></td>
          <td>Reinstalls every component the manifest records.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>theme init</code></td>
          <td>Writes <code>santaka-theme.css</code> and tells you where to import it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>registry [url]</code></td>
          <td>Lists registry sources, or adds one.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>--dry-run</code> shows what would change and writes nothing, on every command that
      writes. <code>--cwd &lt;dir&gt;</code> runs against another directory. <code>--overwrite</code>
      and <code>--skip</code> resolve a conflict, and they are the only way past one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Exit codes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Success. Also a dry run, and also "already up to date" — nothing to do is not a failure.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Error: an unusable framework, an unknown component, a refused path, a failed checksum, a registry that would not load.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Conflict. Files already exist and have been edited since the CLI wrote them, or <code>diff</code> found drift.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The distinction is deliberate: <code>2</code> means the CLI stopped because a decision is
      yours, not because something broke. It is the code <code>diff</code> uses in CI to mean
      "installed files no longer match what was installed".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it refuses to do</h2>
    <p>
      A registry entry is untrusted input. Its <code>files[].path</code> is a string a third party
      wrote, so it is resolved, normalized, and confirmed to stay inside the target directory before
      anything is written. A path like <code>../../.ssh/authorized_keys</code> is refused, not
      sanitized:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>path "…" contains a traversal segment</code></li>
      <li><code>path "…" resolves outside the target directory</code></li>
      <li><code>path contains a null byte</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>It also refuses to install a file it cannot verify:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;item&gt;: source file &lt;path&gt; is not present in the published package</code></li>
      <li><code>&lt;item&gt;: checksum mismatch for &lt;path&gt; — the published file is not the one the entry describes</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      And it does not overwrite your work. The CLI records the hash of what it wrote in
      <code>santaka-manifest.json</code>; if a file's bytes have changed since, it stops and prints
      what it found:
    </p>
    <pre><code>Nothing was written. Choose one:
  --overwrite   replace the files above with the registry copy
  --skip        leave them and install everything else</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Errors, and what to do about them</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Message</th><th>Cause and fix</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Could not determine a framework.</code></td>
          <td>Nothing in the directory looks like a supported project. Pass <code>--framework</code> with one of the supported names.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Unknown framework "…". Supported: …</code></td>
          <td>The override is not a supported value. The list in the message is the whole set.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Name at least one item: santaka add button dialog</code></td>
          <td><code>add</code> was run with no names.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Unknown item(s): …</code></td>
          <td>The name is not in the registry. The same output lists what is available; <code>list</code> shows it with install state.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>registry &lt;url&gt; returned &lt;status&gt;</code></td>
          <td>A remote registry source did not answer with a usable payload. Check the URL, and that it serves the registry JSON.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>An entry was rejected during validation</td>
          <td>A remote registry's entry does not match <code>registry-item-v1</code>. The rejected entry and the failing pointers are printed; the others still load.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Exit <code>2</code> with a list of paths</td>
          <td>Those files have been edited since they were installed. Pass <code>--skip</code> to keep them, or <code>--overwrite</code> to replace them with the registry copy.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>doctor</code> is the fastest way to see which of these applies: it reports the detected
      framework, package manager, TypeScript, styling, and config files, with the evidence for each,
      and exits non-zero when it finds a problem.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a failed run leaves behind</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing. Writes go through a journal: every file is copied aside before it is replaced, and a
      failure replays the journal backwards. A half-installed component is worse than an uninstalled
      one, because you cannot tell which half you have. The manifest is left untouched on failure
      too, so a failed run is always safe to repeat.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Registry sources</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI ships with the bundled registry and can be pointed at others. A source is a URL to a
      static registry index — the same JSON shape the published registry has, so a private registry
      is the same artifact in another place, not a second format.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sources live in <code>santaka-registries.json</code> in the project. Adding one is a deliberate
      act: the source URL is printed before anything is written, so you know which third party you
      are about to install from.
    </p>
    <p>
      An entry that passes the schema is <em>well-formed</em>, not <em>trusted</em> — validating JSON
      is not reviewing code. Schema validation always uses the copy of <code>registry-item-v1</code>
      that ships with the CLI, never a schema fetched over the network.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-inline-size: 65ch;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
  }
  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    inline-size: 100%;
    margin-block: 1rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
